WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.816
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.116 --> 00:00:08.398
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.698 --> 00:00:14.125
Unit fifteen: Media Literacy. Today you will use 'before' and 'after' + verb-ing.

4
00:00:14.425 --> 00:00:17.228
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:21.376
Part one: words. You will learn twelve new words.

6
00:00:22.217 --> 00:00:23.496
Credible source.

7
00:00:24.483 --> 00:00:27.470
Credible source means information you can trust.

8
00:00:28.083 --> 00:00:30.826
A university website is a credible source.

9
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:34.545
Your turn. Say: Credible source.

10
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:38.703
Credible source.

11
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.625
Fact-checking.

12
00:00:41.617 --> 00:00:44.661
Fact-checking means checking if information is true.

13
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.784
Fact-checking takes only a minute.

14
00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:51.616
Your turn. Say: Fact-checking.

15
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.768
Fact-checking.

16
00:00:57.517 --> 00:00:58.483
Bias.

17
00:00:59.483 --> 00:01:02.330
Bias means an unfair preference for one side.

18
00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:05.172
This article shows clear bias.

19
00:01:05.783 --> 00:01:08.320
Your turn. Say: Bias.

20
00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:12.307
Bias.

21
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:13.947
Clickbait.

22
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.018
Clickbait means a shocking headline made to get clicks.

23
00:01:18.617 --> 00:01:21.223
'You won't believe this!' is clickbait.

24
00:01:21.817 --> 00:01:24.039
Your turn. Say: Clickbait.

25
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:27.843
Clickbait.

26
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:29.898
Misinformation.

27
00:01:30.883 --> 00:01:33.463
Misinformation means false information.

28
00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.607
Misinformation spreads fast online.

29
00:01:37.217 --> 00:01:40.148
Your turn. Say: Misinformation.

30
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.384
Misinformation.

31
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:46.348
Critical consumer.

32
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.161
Critical consumer means someone who questions what they read and watch.

33
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:53.924
Be a critical consumer of news.

34
00:01:54.517 --> 00:01:57.773
Your turn. Say: Critical consumer.

35
00:02:00.633 --> 00:02:02.052
Critical consumer.

36
00:02:02.817 --> 00:02:04.317
Media influence.

37
00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:09.487
Media influence means the power of media to change opinions.

38
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:12.838
Media influence shapes what teenagers buy.

39
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.718
Your turn. Say: Media influence.

40
00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:21.264
Media influence.

41
00:02:22.017 --> 00:02:23.041
Fake news.

42
00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.199
Fake news means false stories that look like real news.

43
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:30.816
That story about a two-headed fish was fake news.

44
00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.941
Your turn. Say: Fake news.

45
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:37.801
Fake news.

46
00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:39.539
Verify.

47
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.142
Verify means to check that something is true.

48
00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.900
Verify the date before you share.

49
00:02:46.517 --> 00:02:49.018
Your turn. Say: Verify.

50
00:02:51.867 --> 00:02:53.007
Verify.

51
00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:54.763
Objective.

52
00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:58.632
Objective means based on facts, not feelings.

53
00:02:59.217 --> 00:03:01.297
A good reporter stays objective.

54
00:03:01.883 --> 00:03:04.779
Your turn. Say: Objective.

55
00:03:07.633 --> 00:03:08.588
Objective.

56
00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:10.398
Subjective.

57
00:03:11.383 --> 00:03:14.254
Subjective means based on personal feelings.

58
00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.617
Movie reviews are subjective.

59
00:03:17.217 --> 00:03:19.730
Your turn. Say: Subjective.

60
00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:23.777
Subjective.

61
00:03:24.517 --> 00:03:25.564
Perspective.

62
00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.457
Perspective means a point of view.

63
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.293
Read news from more than one perspective.

64
00:03:31.883 --> 00:03:34.884
Your turn. Say: Perspective.

65
00:03:37.733 --> 00:03:38.885
Perspective.

66
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:43.548
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:03:44.383 --> 00:03:47.428
Visal, how can you tell if a news source is credible?

68
00:03:48.517 --> 00:03:52.760
I check who wrote it. I verify facts before sharing anything.

69
00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.815
What about shocking headlines?

70
00:03:56.917 --> 00:04:01.485
That's often clickbait. After reading it, I search other sources.

71
00:04:02.583 --> 00:04:04.702
Good! Is all news objective?

72
00:04:05.817 --> 00:04:12.289
No. Some is subjective or shows bias, so I compare perspectives before believing it.

73
00:04:13.433 --> 00:04:14.934
Part three: grammar.

74
00:04:15.783 --> 00:04:18.723
Grammar focus: before and after plus verb-ing.

75
00:04:19.023 --> 00:04:20.280
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:20.983 --> 00:04:22.936
Verify facts before sharing.

77
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.896
Reflect after reading the news.

78
00:04:26.783 --> 00:04:29.329
Before clicking a link, check the website.

79
00:04:30.267 --> 00:04:34.762
After 'before' and 'after', use the -ing form when the subject is the same.

80
00:04:35.517 --> 00:04:39.411
Be careful! Don't say: Verify facts before share.

81
00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:43.831
Say: Verify facts before sharing.

82
00:04:44.131 --> 00:04:46.478
After 'before' and 'after', use the -ing form.

83
00:04:47.267 --> 00:04:49.907
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.107
Think Before You Share.

85
00:04:52.407 --> 00:04:55.614
Misinformation and fake news spread quickly online.

86
00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.878
Fact-checking helps me avoid clickbait.

87
00:04:59.867 --> 00:05:03.817
Before sharing a story, I verify it with a credible source.

88
00:05:04.833 --> 00:05:08.226
After reading the news, I think about bias and perspective.

89
00:05:09.233 --> 00:05:13.195
Being a critical consumer means verifying sources before sharing news.

90
00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:18.162
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:05:19.017 --> 00:05:21.028
Verify facts before blank.

92
00:05:21.328 --> 00:05:24.505
A, sharing. B, share. C, shared.

93
00:05:28.067 --> 00:05:29.788
The answer is A: sharing.

94
00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.484
A shocking headline made to get clicks is blank.

95
00:05:33.784 --> 00:05:37.402
A, clickbait. B, bias. C, a source.

96
00:05:40.967 --> 00:05:43.059
The answer is A: clickbait.

97
00:05:44.217 --> 00:05:46.832
Based on facts, not feelings, is blank.

98
00:05:47.132 --> 00:05:51.006
A, objective. B, subjective. C, biased.

99
00:05:54.567 --> 00:05:56.450
The answer is A: objective.

100
00:05:57.667 --> 00:06:00.865
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:06:01.717 --> 00:06:04.297
How can you tell if a news source is credible?

102
00:06:13.083 --> 00:06:14.502
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:06:14.802 --> 00:06:21.925
I check who wrote the article and when. Before sharing it, I compare it with other trusted news websites.

104
00:06:22.225 --> 00:06:24.073
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:24.983 --> 00:06:29.934
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and before and after plus verb-ing.

106
00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:34.710
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

107
00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.557
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

108
00:06:37.857 --> 00:06:41.171
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

